--- Rosetta Code: Quoting constructs
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Quoting_constructs
--- Every quoting construct Crow has, each shown with what it prints. Text has
--- one quote, the single quote; inside it backticks hold code (interpolation)
--- and everything else is inert. Other data is written as literals (numbers,
--- truths, lists, maps, records) or read from text by a function (json,
--- decimal, moment, float): there is no second string syntax, no raw string or
--- here-document, no character literal, and no quoting of code.

use bits

struct Point [x, y]

--- 1. Text has one quote. A double-quoted "text" is refused with a message
---    naming the single quote, so no program mixes two styles.
log['plain text in single quotes']

--- 2. Six escapes, each a backslash and one character: \' \\ \` \t \n \r.
log['a quote \', a backslash \\, a backtick \`, a tab \t| and a line break\nhere']

--- A backslash before any other character is kept as written, so a regular
--- expression is written as it is, its backslashes not doubled.
log['\d+ is written as typed']
log[matches['Route 66', '\d+']]

--- 3. Interpolation: backticks in a string hold any expression, shown as log
---    shows its value. Outside the backticks a string is inert: braces, dollar
---    and percent signs and double quotes are just characters.
name = 'Crow'
n = 7
log['`name` has `len[name]` letters, and `n` squared is `n * n`']
log['inert: {name} ${name} $name %s #{name} "name"']
--- the code between backticks is still inside the string, so a quote there is
--- escaped too
log['shouted: `upper[\'quiet\']`']

--- 4. A string may run across lines: each line break is part of the text, and
---    nothing is trimmed.
verse = 'Once upon a midnight dreary,
    while I pondered, weak and weary'
log[verse]

--- 5. Text is Unicode, of any length; len counts characters, not bytes.
word = 'naïve café ✓'
log['`word` has `len[word]` characters']

--- 6. JSON, HTML, SQL and regular expressions go into a string as they are,
---    since only a quote, a backtick and a backslash mean anything there.
doc = json['{"name": "crow", "tags": ["fast", "small"], "year": 2026}']
log['`doc.name`, `doc.tags[1]`, `doc.year`']
log['<a href="https://rosettacode.org/">Rosetta Code</a>']
log['SELECT name FROM birds WHERE kind = \'crow\'']
--- and back: encode writes a value as JSON text, str as log shows it
log[encode[map[say = 'it\'s', tags = '"a" and \\']]]
log[str[map[a = [1, 2]]]]

--- 7. Numbers are literals: an int has as many digits as it is written with,
---    a float a point with digits on both sides. There is no exponent, hex,
---    octal or underscore form, and a minus is an operator: text in any other
---    form is read as a number by float or int.
log[123456789012345678901234567890 * 10]
log[2 ^ 100]
log[3.25]
log[-0.5]
log[float['6.02e23']]
log[int['-42'] + 1]

--- 8. Exact numbers and moments are made by name, from text or from ints.
log[decimal['0.10'] + decimal['0.20']]
log[fraction[1, 3] + fraction[1, 6]]
log[moment['2026-07-14T13:30:00Z'] + 3600]

--- 9. Truths, lists, matrices, maps and records are literals. A list holds one
---    kind, and a nested literal of numbers is a matrix, so its rows are of one
---    length (lists of other lengths are built with push); a map holds values
---    of one kind, its keys names, quoted text or ints; a record holds any.
log[true and not false]
log[[1, 2, 3]]
log[[[1, 2], [3, 4]]]
log[[['a'], ['b', 'c']]]
log[map[crow = 3, 'two words' = 4]]
log[map[1 = 'one', 2 = 'two']]
log[Point[3, 4]]

--- 10. There is no character type: a character is text of length one, its
---     code read with code and made with char (use bits).
a = code['A']
quote = char[39]
log['A is code `a`, and code 39 is the quote `quote`']

--- 11. A comment starts with three dashes and ends with its line; there is no
---     block comment, and nothing in a comment is read.
